### Reasoning

    The problem requires finding the number of ways to swap two elements in a given sequence to make it "nice". A nice sequence has the property that elements at odd indices are strictly increasing and elements at even indices are strictly decreasing. The input sequence is guaranteed to not be nice.

    The approach involves iterating through all possible pairs of indices (i, j) with i < j, swapping the elements at those indices, and then checking if the resulting sequence is nice. If it is, we increment a counter. Finally, we return the counter.

    To check if a sequence is nice, we iterate through the sequence and verify the increasing/decreasing conditions. For odd indices (i = 1, 3, 5,...), we check if the element at index i is less than the element at index i+1. For even indices (i = 2, 4, 6,...), we check if the element at index i is greater than the element at index i+1. If any of these conditions is violated, the sequence is not nice.

