### Reasoning

The problem asks us to determine if a given contamination grid can be produced by a rectangular lair. The contamination level of a cell is the number of corners of the rectangle that are inside or on the border of the cell.

We can iterate through all possible rectangles within the grid and check if the contamination levels generated by that rectangle match the input grid.  A rectangle is defined by two points (x1, y1) and (x2, y2), where x1 < x2 and y1 < y2.

For each cell (x, y) in the grid, we check if the rectangle's corners are within or on the borders of that cell.  A corner (x_corner, y_corner) is inside/on the cell (x, y) if x <= x_corner < x+1 and y <= y_corner < y+1.

We can optimize this by realizing that any cell with contamination level 0 must be completely outside the rectangle, and any cell with contamination level 4 must contain all four corners. We can first check if this is possible, and if so, we proceed to check the contamination levels for all other cells.

